---
import LearnArticle from "../../components/LearnArticle.astro";
import BitChip from "../../components/BitChip.astro";
import BinaryCounter from "../../components/BinaryCounter.astro";

function countingRows(bitLength: number, start: number, end: number) {
	return Array.from({ length: end - start + 1 }, (_, i) => {
		const decimal = start + i;
		return { decimal, bits: decimal.toString(2).padStart(bitLength, "0") };
	});
}

const twoBitRows = countingRows(2, 0, 3);
const threeBitRowsLeft = countingRows(3, 0, 3);
const threeBitRowsRight = countingRows(3, 4, 7);
---

<LearnArticle
	href="/learn/counting-in-binary"
	description="Learn how binary counting actually works — the rollover trick that turns 1 into 10, and 11 into 100 — one step at a time."
>
	<Fragment slot="lead">Binary counting isn't different math — it's the same idea, with fewer symbols. Let's go slowly.</Fragment>

	<section class="block block--example">
		<p class="block-title">Start With Just One Digit</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			In binary, you only ever have two symbols: <BitChip value="0" /> and <BitChip value="1" />. That's
			it.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">With one digit, you can count exactly two things:</p>

		<div class="intro-tiles">
			<div class="intro-group">
				<BitChip value="0" />
				<span class="intro-label">zero</span>
			</div>
			<div class="intro-group">
				<BitChip value="1" />
				<span class="intro-label">one</span>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="reveal-row">
			<span class="reveal-number">0</span>
			<span class="reveal-arrow">→</span>
			<span class="reveal-number">1</span>
		</div>

		<p class="block-text">
			That's your first binary count. You just ran out of digits — there's no "2" in binary. So what
			happens next?
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">The Rollover</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Think about counting on your fingers. You can count 0 through 9 using all ten digits on both
			hands. But what happens when you need to count past 9? You run out of fingers — so you keep
			track of how many "full hands" of ten you've counted, and start over on your fingers for the
			rest.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Binary works the same way, it just runs out of room much sooner — after just 1, not after 9. So
			the next number after <span class="mono-highlight">1</span> isn't <span class="mono-highlight"
				>2</span
			>. It's:
		</p>

		<div class="reveal-row">
			<span class="reveal-number">10</span>
		</div>

		<p class="block-text">
			That "1" on the left isn't a new mystery digit — it's a way of keeping track of "one full group,"
			the same way you'd track "one full set of ten fingers." In binary, each group is just two, not
			ten.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block block--example">
		<p class="block-title">Counting to Three</p>

		<div class="counting-rows">
			{
				twoBitRows.map((row) => (
					<div class="counting-row">
						<span class="counting-tiles">
							{row.bits.split("").map((bit) => (
								<BitChip value={bit as "0" | "1"} />
							))}
						</span>
						<span class="counting-eq">=</span>
						<span class="counting-decimal">{row.decimal}</span>
					</div>
				))
			}
		</div>

		<p class="block-text">
			Notice: every single count, only the rightmost digit changes — 0 to 1 to 0 to 1 — except when it
			rolls over, which bumps the column next to it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">The Tricky Part: What If Every Digit Is Already 1?</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			This is where it usually gets confusing, so let's slow down. Say you're at <span
				class="mono-highlight">11</span
			> (three) and you count one more.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			The rightmost 1 rolls over to 0 — same as before. But now the next column is also already 1, so it
			rolls over too, and bumps the column after that.
		</p>

		<div class="reveal-row">
			<span class="reveal-number">11</span>
			<span class="reveal-arrow">→</span>
			<span class="reveal-number">100</span>
		</div>

		<p class="block-text">
			It's the same idea as running out of fingers on both hands at once — you'd need a new way to
			track it, and a new column appears. Binary does this constantly, since it only takes one
			rollover to trigger the next.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block block--example">
		<p class="block-title">Counting to Seven</p>

		<div class="counting-columns">
			<div class="counting-col">
				{
					threeBitRowsLeft.map((row) => (
						<div class="counting-row">
							<span class="counting-tiles">
								{row.bits.split("").map((bit) => (
									<BitChip value={bit as "0" | "1"} />
								))}
							</span>
							<span class="counting-eq">=</span>
							<span class="counting-decimal">{row.decimal}</span>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>
			<div class="counting-col">
				{
					threeBitRowsRight.map((row) => (
						<div class="counting-row">
							<span class="counting-tiles">
								{row.bits.split("").map((bit) => (
									<BitChip value={bit as "0" | "1"} />
								))}
							</span>
							<span class="counting-eq">=</span>
							<span class="counting-decimal">{row.decimal}</span>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>
		</div>

		<p class="block-text">
			Look closely at the rightmost column — it alternates every single time: 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1. The
			next column over changes half as often. The one after that, half as often again. That pattern
			keeps going, no matter how many digits you add.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block block--example">
		<p class="block-title">Try It Yourself</p>
		<BinaryCounter />
		<p class="block-text try-it-caption">
			Press +1 a bunch of times in a row and watch what happens once you reach a run of 1s — you'll see
			the rollover happen live.
		</p>
	</section>

</LearnArticle>

<style>

	.block--example .block-text {
		text-align: left;
	}

	.try-it-caption {
		text-align: center;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}

	/* The 1-bit intro: two labeled tiles side by side, same shape as
	   bits-and-bytes.astro's .byte-demo but with a text label ("zero"/"one")
	   instead of a bit-count label, since the point here is naming the
	   values, not counting bits. */
	.intro-tiles {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 2rem;
		margin: 1rem 0;
		padding: 1rem;
		border-radius: 12px;
		background: var(--color-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.intro-group {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}

	.intro-group :global(.bit-chip) {
		width: 2.2rem;
		height: 2.2rem;
	}

	.intro-label {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	/* A single "before → after" (or standalone) number call-out, used for
	   every rollover reveal on this page (0 → 1, the bare 10, 11 → 100) —
	   the same glowing-amber treatment as .counting-decimal below, just
	   sized up since here the number itself IS the point of the block,
	   not a small annotation next to a row of tiles. */
	.reveal-row {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 1rem;
		margin: 1rem 0;
		padding: 0.75rem;
		border-radius: 12px;
		background: var(--color-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.reveal-number {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1.8rem;
		color: #fff3d6;
		text-shadow: 0 0 8px color-mix(in srgb, #ffae2e 60%, transparent);
	}

	.reveal-arrow {
		color: #ffae2e;
		font-size: 1.4rem;
		line-height: 1;
	}

	.counting-rows {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(7rem, 1fr));
		gap: 0.5rem 0.5rem;
		margin: 1rem 0;
		padding: 1rem;
		border-radius: 12px;
		background: var(--color-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	/* Counting to Seven: two explicit columns (0-3, then 4-7) rather than
	   one auto-fit grid — the point of this table is that the LEADING bit
	   is 0 for the whole left half and 1 for the whole right half, which
	   only reads clearly if those two groups stay visually separate
	   columns instead of just wrapping wherever the row happens to fill. */
	.counting-columns {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: center;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
		margin: 1rem 0;
		padding: 1rem;
		border-radius: 12px;
		background: var(--color-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.counting-col {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}

	.counting-row {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}

	.counting-tiles {
		display: inline-flex;
		gap: 0.25rem;
	}

	.counting-eq {
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.counting-decimal {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
		color: #fff3d6;
		text-shadow: 0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, #ffae2e 60%, transparent);
	}

</style>
